INTRODUCTION
1.1Background to the Study
Mental health issues are widespread today, affecting people from all walks of life (WHO, 2023). The World Health Organization (WHO, 2023) says that almost one in eight people worldwide deals with a mental health condition, with anxiety and depression being the most common. These issues negatively impact individual well-being and contribute to the global disease burden. A troubling number of people don't seek help for their mental health. This often stems from the stigma surrounding mental illness, a shortage of local mental health experts, the high cost of treatment, and the difficulty to get care. 
To solve some of these problems, new technology  has stepped in to make access to mental healthcare easier. AI chatbots are one example, providing remote psychological support. Mishra and Ajwani (2022) stated these technologies offer constant availability, confidentiality, and affordability, tackling some of the shortcomings of traditional therapy. These chatbots use Natural Language Processing (NLP) and machine learning to understand what people say and offer caring, relevant responses in real-time (Farzan et al., 2021).
Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) is as good starting point for these smart systems. It is easy to add treatment to chatbots because it is organized and fact-based. CBT chatbots can copy therapist-client chats, guiding people through things like changing their thoughts, tracking their moods, and taking action. This helps people detect and handle negative thoughts and feelings (Joshi et al., 2023). 
AI can help with mental health, but current chatbot systems are not flawless.  They may be better because their system breaks down when it needs to create custom solutions and handle sensitive information and manage urgent situations.  Some chatbots deliver general answers that do not properly address each user's emotional requirements, which undermines their therapeutic efficacy.  Those technologies become less effective because people stop using them when their capabilities fail to provide instant adjustments.  The situation creates a sense despair for people who experience their most difficult times.
1.2.  Statement of the Problem
The use of artificial intelligence in mental health care has led to the creation of novel methods for offering psychological support.  Despite these advantages, modern chatbots using artificial intelligence technologies have numerous fundamental drawbacks, which impair their usefulness.  For instance, these chatbots can only deliver broad responses, which do not meet the emotional requirements of consumers, hence diminishing their usefulness. In addition, modern chatbots using artificial intelligence technology cannot react to user needs in real time, nor do they have necessary mechanisms to ensure their users’ emotional well-being.  For instance, many chatbots do not allow users to communicate with them through other ways apart from text, which decreases their efficacy.  As a result, users may quit using these chatbots or not obtain the care they need, especially during emergencies. These restrictions call for the invention of a smart, adaptable, and moral artificial intelligence system that can give a cognitive behavioral support structure without sacrificing user trust and privacy.
1.3.	Aim and Objectives of the Study
The study aimed to design and develop an AI-powered chatbot therapist, which can deliver Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT)-based guidance and support to patients confronting mild or moderate levels of emotional discomfort.
 To achieve the goals and objectives of this research, the following particular objectives was outlined: 
i.To identify and understand the existing psychological difficulties and discourses which can be addressed and improved utilizing Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) interventions. 
ii. To design a user-friendly chatbot that can engage people in a discussion and interact empathetically and therapeutically through Natural Language Processing (NLP).  
iii.To build a chatbot therapist that can deliver Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) treatments and reply effectively and adequately to users' interactions using rule-based talks and sentiment analysis.  
iv.To deploy the chatbot therapist on a web-based platform to target individuals who need mental health support and self-therapy 
v.To test and measure the performance of the chatbot therapist in terms of emotional accuracy and response speed.
1.4	Scope of the Study
The AI powered CBT Chatbot is an example of an instrument that is digital in form and tries to promote Cognitive Behavioral Therapy to users in the form of a chat-based interface.  The AI CBT chatbot employs natural language processing and sentiment analysis to aid persons with depression in a structured dialogue-based connection.  The project also tackles ethical issues such as data protection and responsible AI usage to verify that it fulfills specified psychological and technological norms.
1.5	Methodology Overview
The project followed a planned process to reach its purpose of producing an AI chatbot therapist which uses Cognitive Behavior Therapy (CBT) methodologies for treatment.   The initial phase entailed researching scientific literature to find typical psychological challenges that customers experience and CBT-based chatbot designs.   The second phase focused on creating the chatbot and applying NLP to allow the chatbot to interpret user input and support a dialog that demonstrates caring and awareness of users.   The third goal includes constructing the chatbot to integrate logic-based rules with sentiment analysis and ethical data collecting to ensure that the chatbot answers in accordance with CBT principles and users’ emotional states.   The final step developed a web application which users could access through any device that had internet connection while the team focused on designing an interface that was easy to use and protective of user information.   The final phase comprises testing the chatbot with users to measure accuracy in responding to users and their contentment with the responses.
1.6	Significance of the Study
The AI powered CBT chatbot system would be easily available for mental health aid.  It will provide tailored and confidential therapy 24/7 to persons battling emotional challenges.  This chatbot will offer flexible and cheap mental health therapy.  It does this by replicating set CBT.  The plan reduces down on the need for in-person therapy by removing things like stigma, travel, and a shortage of health pros.  Sentiment analysis makes possible quick and emotionally right advice and the care of privacy puts ethics and user privacy at the front.  So, this approach improves access to mental health treatment for all and drives growth in tech-based mental health aid.
