2.3.	 Mental Health
Mental health covers a person's emotions, mind, and social life. It impacts how people see themselves, get along with others, handle stress, and make choices. Good mental health is more than just not being sick, it also involves things like being able to bounce back, control emotions, feel good about oneself, and have good relationships. (World Health Organization, 2022).
According to the World Health Organization (2022), mental health is defined as “a state of mental well-being that enables people to cope with the stresses of life, realize their abilities, learn well and work well, and contribute to their communities.” This definition highlights not only personal functioning but also the broader capacity to participate meaningfully in society. It underscores the interdependence between individual wellness and societal productivity.
Several factors influence mental health, including:
Biological factors, such as genetics and brain chemistry
Psychological influences, including trauma, early childhood experiences, or personality patterns
Social and environmental determinants, such as poverty, discrimination, isolation, or limited access to support systems
Mental health should be viewed as a spectrum, not as simply healthy or ill. People can struggle with their mental health, even if they don't have a diagnosis, especially when stressed or going through big life changes. Acknowledging this range permits a wider, more realistic view on mental well-being.


2.3.2.	 Common Mental Health Conditions
 Mental health disorders create challenges which damage people's ability to control their emotions and their cognitive abilities and their ability to conduct themselves. The conditions present as mild disturbances which develop into chronic conditions that disrupt daily life and damage personal relationships and decrease work efficiency. Depression and anxiety disorders and stress-related syndromes rank as the most common medical conditions throughout the world which public health officials now treat as critical health issues affecting entire populations (World Health Organization 2022).
Depression
Depression serves as a major mental health disorder which affects people throughout the world. The condition involves more than a temporary state of sadness which lasts. The symptoms of depression lead people to experience constant hopelessness and a complete loss of interest in activities which they used to find enjoyable and extreme tiredness and difficulties with focus and a decline in their self-esteem. People may develop suicidal thoughts or engage in suicidal actions during extreme situations. The new digital therapy methods show effectiveness to reduce depressive symptoms because they provide instant access to mental health support (Stanford et al. 2024).
Anxiety Disorder
Anxiety disorders create excessive fear and worry which lead to major disruptions in everyday activities. The three primary subtypes consist of Generalized Anxiety Disorder and Panic Disorder and Social Anxiety Disorder. The symptoms usually include six specific signs which are restlessness and sleep difficulties and racing thoughts and muscle tension and rapid heart rate. The conditions develop because people experience continuous stress and personal difficulties and they face dangerous situations. Review evidence indicates that AI-powered chatbots can reduce anxiety symptoms, offering emotional reassurance, cognitive reframing, and relaxation prompts (Lis et al., 2024).
Stress-Related Conditions
Humans develop a natural stress response which helps them face challenges but persistent stress without management results in harmful effects on both mental and physical well-being. The body experiences chronic stress which leads to irritability, fatigue, concentration problems, sleep disturbances and reduced immune system function. Digital tools especially those incorporating AI-guided interventions can help people with stress management by providing them with personalized coping methods and techniques for controlling their emotions and they receive immediate assistance (Ben Yehuda et al., 2024)
2.3.3.	 Challenges in Accessing Mental Health Support
People who understand mental health requirements continue to face barriers which stop them from getting essential support. The mental health requirements of both rich and poor countries remain unfulfilled because these requirements emerge from personal and social and cultural and systemic factors. The process of identifying these obstacles needs to take place because it enables us to create mental health solutions which maintain their effectiveness for all users while delivering equal treatment. Figure 2.1 illustrates a multi-layered model of barriers to accessing mental health care.
Stigma and Social Attitudes
 Stigma stands as a major obstacle to mental health support. Mental health issues receive association with weakness and failure in numerous territories. People develop shame about their problems because these ideas prevent them from showing their actual feelings. People avoid receiving help because they fear being judged by others. People delay treatment because they ignore their symptoms which problem internalized negative beliefs (Clement et al., 2015).
Limited Availability of Mental Health Professionals 
The global shortage of qualified mental health professionals continues to restrict access, particularly in low-income, rural, and underserved communities. People in these regions need to deal with extended waiting times and travel challenges because there is insufficient access to specialized treatment. People in developed areas face service distribution problems which prevent them from accessing mental healthcare services according to their geographical location or financial situation (AAMC, 2022; Economic Times, 2025).


Financial Barriers 
Affordability stands as the main barrier which people need to overcome. The cost of mental health services, including therapy sessions, psychiatric evaluations, and medication, can be prohibitive for many individuals. People in situations where mental health care does not get health insurance coverage need to pay all their expenses which prevents them from starting or continuing treatment.

