2.4.	 Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT)
Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) is a well-supported and frequently used therapy in mental health. It's based on the idea that our thoughts about events, not just the events themselves, cause mental distress (Jiang et al., 2024). CBT helps people change their thinking to improve their feelings and actions, which improves their mental state (Farzan et al., 2021).
CBT differs from older therapies that look into childhood or unconscious reasons for problems. CBT focuses on the present and aims to make people aware of their thoughts and change negative thinking patterns. This makes CBT useful for people who want practical steps and clear progress (Beck, 2011).
CBT sessions usually include education about mental health, goal setting, changing thoughts, and trying new behaviors. People are asked to practice these things between sessions to build confidence and strengthen what they learn in therapy (Beck, 2011). This organized way to deal with problems works well for conditions like depression, anxiety, panic attacks, obsessive-compulsive disorder, and PTSD (Hofmann et al., 2012).
The logical way CBT is set up has allowed it to be used in computer and AI programs. These tools give people therapy they can do on their own, ways to write down thoughts, track mood, and encourage activity through online sites. Farzan et al. (2021) and Jiang et al. (2024) noted that these programs have especially helped more people get mental health care, mainly those who don't have good access or who use technology often.
2.4.1. 	Origins and Development of CBT
The development of Cognitive Behavioral Therapy began during the 1960s because psychiatrist Aaron T. Beck conducted his fundamental research. Psychoanalysis served as Beck's initial training but he began to doubt its effectiveness when he treated patients suffering from depression. He discovered that his patients experienced automatic negative thoughts together with deep-seated self-perceptions and worldviews and anticipated future events. These ideas helped create a new treatment plan that focused on clear thinking processes (Beck, 2011).
CBT became a therapy that was organized, short-term, and based on proof. The system operated based on the understanding that incorrect thinking results in emotional distress and its associated behavioral problems. Beck's first work showed how spotting and questioning wrong beliefs could make depression symptoms less intense and make people feel better. The shift from hidden reasons to distinct thought patterns brought about a transformation in mental health treatment (Beck, 2011).
CBT development received its primary influence from early behavioral theories which B.F. Skinner and Albert Bandura created. CBT incorporated behavioral therapy techniques such as exposure reinforcement modeling and activity planning to develop methods that transform observable behavior. The system combined these elements with cognitive methods to create a complete solution which addressed both cognitive and behavioral aspects (Skinner, 1953; Bandura, 1977).
The treatment method known as CBT requires scientific evidence for its evaluation because it distinguishes itself from most other treatment methods. The effectiveness of CBT treatment for various mental health disorders which can be treated through this method with different patient groups has been established through multiple controlled studies and extended research evaluations which have followed the treatment since its inception (Butler et al., 2006). This treatment method has become popular because it can be adapted to various clinical settings and community environments and online platforms.
The structure of CBT consists of definite objectives together with assessment methods and client collaborative work which enables its integration with contemporary technological solutions. The program enables organizations to deliver their content through distinct units and online modules which simplifies its incorporation into digital therapy platforms and artificial intelligence mental health systems. The system enables improved patient access while maintaining its effectiveness with contemporary treatment approaches (Farzan et al., 2021).
2.4.3.	 Core Ideas of CBT and Unhelpful Thinking Patterns
The principles of Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) rest upon the belief that thoughts and feelings and actions maintain a strong interrelationship with one another. The way people perceive the world determines their emotional responses and their subsequent behavior according to this theory. When individuals make negative or distorted judgments about situation they experience distress which leads to problematic behavior. The main purpose of CBT is to assist individuals in detecting their thought patterns and transforming them to support better mental health and coping strategies according to Dobson and Dozois 2019. Figure 2.2 illustrates the Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) triad showing the interplay between thoughts, feelings, and behaviors (Beck, 2011).
The central principle of CBT involves the existence of cognitive distortions which refer to automatic thinking patterns that people develop through their daily lives which produce false and excessively pessimistic and irrational thoughts. The human mind creates distorted thought patterns which people maintain without realizing it because these patterns have a powerful effect on their emotional states and their actions. The therapeutic process in CBT involves guiding individuals to identify these distortions, critically evaluate them, and replace them with more balanced, evidence-based perspectives.
The following cognitive distortions represent the most common types of mental errors which people experience in their thinking patterns. 
All-or-Nothing Thinking, which requires people to see all situations through absolute borders, leads them to extreme thinking because they think "If I’m not successful, I’m a complete failure".
 Overgeneralization, which requires people to see all situations through absolute borders, leads them to extreme thinking because they think "If I’m not successful, I’m a complete failure". People who practice Catastrophizing expect that everything will end in total disaster, regardless of how probable or contextual the situation may arise.
 Personalization involves people who assign blame for all external events and other individuals' conduct toward themselves without valid proof. Emotional Reasoning occurs when people believe their emotional state proves their actual circumstances because they feel anxious which leads them to think that dangerous things must be occurring.
The structured techniques of CBT address these patterns through their process which consists of three steps starting with negative thought assessment and proceeding to alternative explanation examination and ending with behavioral testing of assumptions. People experience decreased emotional distress through this process which enables them to develop healthier ways of interpreting their daily life events. 
CBT treatment extends beyond automatic thoughts to core beliefs which represent deep-seated unconscious assumptions about personal identity and social relationships and world perception. The beliefs that people develop during their early life experiences become the basis for their cognitive schema which determines their approach to processing new information. The foundational beliefs which people hold can be revealed and changed through therapeutic dialogue and gradual exploration according to Beck (2011).
Digital therapeutic platforms work effectively with CBT because of its modular design which allows users to progress through treatment at their own pace. The core elements of thought monitoring and cognitive restructuring and behavioral experiments can be successfully implemented through mobile applications and AI-driven chatbots. Interactive exercises with feedback will be delivered through these technologies which make CBT accessible to people who encounter geographic and financial and social obstacles that prevent them from attending in-person therapy sessions according to Wright and his colleagues in 2019.


