2.4.4.  CBT Techniques
Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) uses a range of structured methods to change unhelpful thoughts and behaviors. The techniques researchers developed through scientific research deliver practical results which can be measured while they achieve specific objectives (Hofmann et al., 2012). The structured nature of CBT techniques enables their effective use in therapy as well as their integration into digital mental health solutions which include AI systems and self-help tools (Farzan et al., 2021).
Key CBT techniques include:
Thought Records – The exercise involves users to record their automatic negative thoughts which they experience during distressing situations through guided worksheets. The exercise requires users to assess the evidence that supports or disputes their thoughts while they develop more rational and balanced alternatives. Beck (2011) first introduced thought records as the basic tool for cognitive restructuring which Greenberger and Padesky (2016) later developed into clinical manuals.
Behavioral Activation – The technique treats depression by encouraging patients to participate in activities which they previously avoided because of their depressed mood. The approach aims to break the patterns of inactivity which lead to negative reinforcement while it works to enhance both motivation and emotional well-being.
Cognitive Restructuring - The process requires people to identify their distorted thinking patterns while they evaluate their irrational and excessive thoughts through systematic testing. The process helps individuals develop accurate thoughts through logical reasoning and evidence-based inquiries (Beck 2011). 
Exposure Therapy people use this treatment method for anxiety and trauma-related disorders because it helps them face their most distressing situations. According to Beck (2011) The technique of using repeated exposure leads to desensitization which diminishes both avoidance behavior and emotional responses. 
Problem-Solving Skills Training teaches individuals to approach challenges in a structured way by defining problems clearly brainstorming potential solutions weighing pros and cons and implementing and reviewing outcomes. The NCBI (2021) approach helps people develop their ability to control actual problems which they encounter in their daily lives. 
Relaxation Techniques - The CBT therapy program uses deep breathing and progressive muscle relaxation and guided imagery to help people manage their stress levels. The techniques help people with anxiety by reducing their physical symptoms while they help to control their emotional states. 
Therapists who build relationships with their clients teach them various techniques which they need to learn. The clients select their objectives and complete their tasks while maintaining a record of their progress through active participation in the process. According to Dobson and Dozois 2019 The personal empowerment focus of the skill-building framework enables CBT to achieve its long-term success.
The digital formats of mental health care treatment methods are becoming more popular as the field of mental health treatment progresses. The combination of interactive applications with AI-based platforms enables users to complete cognitive behavioral therapy exercises while receiving educational materials and instant progress evaluation. The new developments maintain core CBT design principles which include both structure and evidence-based treatment methods while making their benefits accessible to people in need who lack proper treatment facilities (Jiang et al., 2024).
