2.5. 	Artificial Intelligence in Mental Health Care
 AI includes different types of computer methods and systems that have been created to copy or expand human mental capabilities which include learning and thinking and problem-solving and sensory perception, as well as language comprehension. The mental healthcare system will experience significant transformation because AI enables customized treatment solutions which healthcare providers can deliver at faster speed than standard healthcare facilities (Cruz-Gonzalez et al., 2025).
AI functions as a technology which enables computers to demonstrate human intelligence through algorithms that power its complete system operations. The system depends on machine learning which permits systems to enhance their performance through learning from data and natural language processing which allows computers to comprehend human language while producing human-readable text. The basic elements of the system function as essential building blocks for various AI applications which help psychologists with evaluations and clinical assessment processes and patient interaction methods (Cruz-Gonzalez et al., 2025).
AI is being used more in healthcare for many things, from figuring out what’s wrong with patients and suggesting what to do to sorting patients and making administrative tasks easier. The mental healthcare field uses AI technology to detect initial signs of emotional disturbances while providing evidence-based treatment methods and online platforms for ongoing patient support (Casu et al., 2024).
AI provides an effective method to deliver anonymous support through therapy chatbots which enable users to access help whenever they need it. The system enables mental healthcare facilities to deliver services to people who lack required treatment resources while reducing existing demand on conventional healthcare systems (Baek, Cha, & Han, 2025). The mental healthcare field needs to establish guidelines which will direct proper AI application in its treatment methods.
2.5.1	 Key AI Technologies in Therapy Chatbots
The current AI-based tools for mental health treatment use multiple artificial intelligence domains which each provide different benefits to their therapeutic systems. The following section presents the main artificial intelligence technologies that researchers utilize for online therapy treatment. Figure 2.3 presents a Venn diagram illustrating the integration of key AI technologies in therapy chatbots.
 The first technology used by online therapy systems is Machine Learning which employs its algorithms to extract patterns from extensive data sets while improving performance without needing new programming. The mental health field uses machine learning to process user data which includes interaction records and mood tracking and sensor measurements and clinical evaluations for behavior pattern detection and relapse prediction and personalized treatment development (Shatte et al., 2019). The supervised learning model decision trees and support vector machines classify users according to their symptom severity while clustering functions as an unsupervised method for identifying hidden patterns within user behavior data.
 Natural Language Processing (NLP) enables digital systems to interpret and generate human language which functions as the core technology for chatbot-based therapies. The Natural Language Processing (NLP) system uses four techniques, which include tokenization and named entity recognition and part-of-speech tagging and intent classification, to enable chatbots to conduct contextually appropriate conversations. The therapeutic dialogue process receives enhancement through sentiment analysis and emotion detection, which enable systems to deliver empathetic responses. Deep learning models have reached new heights of development through the introduction of transformer-based architectures which include both BERT and GPT systems, which has resulted in advanced Natural Language Processing capabilities for mental health applications.
Expert Systems:
Expert systems operate by using established logical rules which they apply to structured knowledge bases to replicate the decision-making abilities of doctors (Russell & Norvig 2021). Mental health care systems use these systems to assist users through established therapy pathways which include CBT exercises and crisis management procedures and psychoeducational treatment methods. The rule-based structure of expert systems provides complete system visibility which allows users to trace system operations while the system maintains definite boundaries because of its fixed rules (Vilaza & McCashin 2021). 

Affective Computing:
Affective computing describes the development of technologies which enable machines to recognize human emotions and interpret them and respond to them. The system processes multiple sources of data which include facial expressions and vocal tone and written language and physiological signals which include heart rate variability and galvanic skin response to determine the user's emotional state in real time. The system uses this capability to create interactive experiences which will detect stress responses and start relaxation exercises. Affective computing establishes direct user connection through its development of human-like treatment spaces that users experience during therapy sessions (McDuff & Czerwinski 2018).

