The AI mental health systems use multiple natural language processing methods to analyze emotional signals and understand user purposes and create suitable responses. These techniques enable chatbots and virtual assistants to deliver responses which maintain correct language usage while reflecting emotional state and therapeutic treatment needs. The main methods of this study encompass these components
Sentiment Analysis: This technique involves identifying the emotional polarity (positive, negative, or neutral) of a user’s message. Sentiment analysis enables therapy applications to identify user moods which include sadness and anger and anxiety (Cambria et al., 2022). The system uses this information to provide users with reassurance and empathetic answers and emotional control suggestions (Calvo et al., 2017).
Intent Recognition: Intent recognition seeks to uncover the purpose behind a user’s message. In mental health contexts the system can determine if a user experiences distress or requires guidance or needs a coping mechanism or wants to express their thoughts. The system achieves accurate intent identification which enables it to provide responses that match user requirements and therapeutic objectives.
 Named Entity Recognition (NER): The NER system detects and categorizes essential textual elements which include personal names, temporal expressions, geographical points and particular issues of interest. The system utilizes therapeutic relationship patterns to identify common themes which include family disputes and educational pressure while it customizes user interactions and detects dangerous content which includes suicidal ideation and abuse indications. 
Emotion Detection: The emotion detection system identifies specific emotional states which include fear, guilt, hopelessness and joy. The system uses its detailed understanding of emotions to determine suitable response methods and treatment options while creating stronger empathetic connections with users.
Text Classification and Topic Modeling: This method enables users to classify their input into specific established categories or subject areas. A user who shows irrational beliefs should receive guidance to the cognitive restructuring module, while a user who describes behavioral avoidance should proceed to the behavioral activation exercises (Blei et al., 2003). The system uses classification methods to create customized Cognitive Behavioral Therapy solutions which follow a standardized structure.
Context Tracking: The therapeutic process requires therapists to maintain a consistent flow of dialogue across different segments of their session. The NLP models employ memory systems together with contextual embedding technology to maintain user input from previous interactions in their system memory, which enables them to develop targeted answers (Serban et al., 2016). The system provides users with better social connection abilities and enables them to develop authentic, continuous conversations.
