2.6.3.	 Dialogue Management in Therapy Chatbots
AI systems use dialogue management to organize their interactions with users through structured conversation patterns which maintain logical flow between different dialogue segments. The mental health chatbots need proper dialogue management systems which protect therapeutic contact between users and therapists while displaying emotional understanding to accomplish their psychological treatment goals.
Dialogue management enables chatbots to deliver appropriate responses through different therapeutic methods which include mood control and cognitive restructuring and psychoeducation delivery. The system Needs user intent comprehension together with previous interaction memory tracking to create real-time updates of user emotions during their entire dialogue process.
Dialogue State Tracking
Dialogue state tracking serves as a fundamental element of dialogue management because it enables the chatbot to sustain uninterrupted communication throughout multiple user sessions or within one current session. Through cognitive restructuring prompts the system enables users who disclose distressing thoughts to receive appropriate follow-up support. The system enables users to continue their mood tracking process from the precise moment they stopped. Users develop a feeling of being listened to which strengthens their trust and relationship with the therapist.
Response Generation and Selection
The two primary methods which chatbots use to create their answers includes: 
Rule-based techniques use predefined templates which activate when users enter specific commands. 
The generative techniques use large language models such as GPT to create new responses which fit the existing situation. 
The mixed approach works best in therapeutic environments because it combines two effective methods. The system uses rule-based responses to generate predictable outputs while generative models produce human-like dialogue which ensures safe clinical operations. The chatbots use these methods to provide appropriate empathetic responses while they eliminate dangerous or irrelevant content (AlMakinah et al., 2024).
Personalization and Adaptation
The process of dialogue management needs to create personalized experiences which use information from particular users to develop unique conversational paths. The system should create customized answers based on the user's past emotional states and most common discussion themes and typical health issues. The system uses social anxiety symptoms to identify which treatment methods users need during specific situations. When users get customized content through their interactions with the chatbot personalized material provides better experiences which match their individual needs.
Goal-Oriented Conversations
The therapy chatbots work with specific objectives which differ from the open-ended conversation design found in regular chatbots. The main objective of their work requires them to assist users in reaching specific psychological goals which include anxiety reduction and depressive symptom control and self-awareness development. Through its management of dialogue between users and therapists the system conducts brief interactions which help users achieve their therapy objectives.

Managing Therapeutic Structure
Certain therapy chatbots operate according to established psychotherapeutic methods which include CBT as their fundamental treatment method. The dialogue management system enables users to progress through predefined procedures of the system which encompass automatic thought identification and cognitive distortion assessment and behavior activation task execution. The structured method of treatment delivery replicates conventional therapy methods which helps users become accustomed to treatment while improving its effectiveness.
2.6.4. Challenges and Limitations of NLP in Therapy
Natural Language Processing (NLP) creates multiple challenges that prevent its successful implementation in mental health applications despite its ability to deliver revolutionary outcomes. The AI-driven interventions which depend on user interaction face three main threats when users experience their most sensitive emotional times and their most critical mental health situations.
Emotional Subtlety and Context Sensitivity
Therapeutic dialogue contains human language which displays various emotional states through its complex use of musicality and unspoken meaning. Users may express distress through sarcasm, slang, metaphors, or culturally specific idioms. The advanced models of NLP systems face difficulties understanding these particular subtle features which results in their inability to produce suitable responses that maintain user trust while controlling distress.
Risk Detection and Crisis Management
The accurate identification of high-risk situations which include suicidal thoughts and self-harm and extreme depression remains a major challenge for specialists. Artificial intelligence solutions create false negatives (which fail to identify dangerous signals) and false positives (which incorrectly identify harmless statements as hazardous) both of which produce dangerous outcomes. The system contains emergency escalation protocols but their effectiveness depends on users' understanding of the system's language.
Linguistic and Cultural Bias
NLP systems develop their training models through datasets which fail to include all languages and dialects and cultural expressions. The system creates misinterpretations about linguistically diverse users who belong to marginalized groups because it fails to understand their language which results in improper answers that decrease treatment effectiveness while creating a negative impact on their mental health.
Limitations in Empathy and Human-Like Understanding 
Empathy capacity and human-like comprehension abilities remain restricted. Modern NLP systems generate empathetic responses yet they lack the ability to experience genuine human emotions. The system produces synthetic empathy which fails to deliver genuine emotional support during high-stress situations thus creating limited therapeutic relationships with users. 
Privacy and Ethical Concerns
The therapy chatbots handle extremely private information which makes privacy concerns the most important issue. The process requires thorough solutions to address three main issues which include obtaining informed consent, protecting data securely, and maintaining user privacy. The absence of proper protective measures allows unauthorized access to data which results in trust violations and causes users to discontinue their treatment. 
Technical Limitations and Model Errors
NLP systems still produce incorrect results because their development has not reached complete scientific maturity. The therapeutic process faces interruptions because users exhibit two problems which include misinterpreting emotional expressions and failing to convey their actual needs. The system will create errors that remain undetected because human supervisors cannot check its operations.
2.7.	 Chatbots in Mental Health Support
Chatbots function as software systems which create humanlike conversation experiences through text and voice interaction methods. In mental health contexts, they function as digital companions that deliver emotional support, facilitate psychoeducation, and, in some cases, administer structured therapeutic interventions. The increasing adoption of their technology in digital mental health treatment emerges from Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Natural Language Processing (NLP) advancements which facilitate more complex human behavior analysis and response creation (Cho et al., 2023).
Chatbots develop their interactive systems because they identify user inputs and process them by using either rule-based systems or machine learning models to deliver contextually relevant responses. The effectiveness of therapeutic treatment depends on how well the chatbot's basic design and operational framework function (Jovanović et al., 2020).
Table 2.2 shows that mental health applications use three main chatbot types which include rule-based systems and AI-powered systems and hybrid systems.
The operation of rule-based chatbots depends on their use of pre-established scripts together with decision trees that implement basic "if-then" rules. These systems demonstrate dependable performance which protects users during their execution of basic functions that include delivering psychoeducational material and assisting users with their mood assessment process. The system becomes unable to handle complicated emotional discussions because it fails to provide users with the necessary tools to handle advanced interactive scenarios.
AI-powered chatbots use machine learning plus natural language processing to understand user input which allows them to create more authentic spoken dialogue. The systems use AI to recognize user emotional states through their natural speaking style while they try to understand their underlying communication goals. The system produces unpredictable clinical content because it lacks proper safety measures which need human supervision for better control of its output.
