The classification of chatbots according to their logical interaction patterns enables their division into two distinct categories which include text-based systems and voice-based systems. Text-based chatbots dominate mental health applications, as they foster self-reflection, allow users to describe their emotions in written form, and preserve a sense of privacy. Voice-based chatbots enable users to engage without using their hands while giving a more natural human experience, although they present challenges about data security that arise in public and shared areas. The selection and deployment of a chatbot type should therefore fit with the desired therapeutic goals, user preferences, and ethical considerations surrounding mental health treatment. A well-designed chatbot enables users to control their emotions and change their behavior while establishing user trust through its adherence to privacy protection and its ability to exhibit understanding and deliver prompt answers.
2.7.2.	 Structure and Features of Mental Health Chatbots
Mental health chatbots are complex digital systems carefully engineered to simulate supportive conversations, deliver psychological interventions, and foster emotional well-being. These systems are built by combining principles from psychotherapy especially evidence-based models like Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) with advanced software engineering, Artificial Intelligence (AI), and Natural Language Processing (NLP) (Kalinin, 2025). Their structure reflects both technological sophistication and therapeutic intent, ensuring that users receive not just automated responses but meaningful mental health support.
At the heart of a mental health chatbot lies a coordinated architecture comprising several interdependent components:
User Interface (UI): The UI functions as the system that enables users to interact with the chatbot. The interface needs to provide users with an easy-to-use design, which shows understanding of their needs, and allows everyone to use it on mobile applications and websites and messaging services. Users will trust the system and use it more often because its user interface design establishes credibility which enables users to maintain their therapy sessions (Kalinin, 2025).
Dialogue Management Engine: The component controls how the chatbot will carry out its conversations. The system uses user input and its internal rules to decide which interactions it will display next. Dialogue engines may follow a rule-based model, leverage AI-based learning, or adopt a hybrid configuration. The dialogue engine in mental health applications needs to provide responses which match the situation while showing proper emotional response and delivering helpful treatment (Chen et al., 2024).
Natural Language Processing (NLP) Module: NLP technologies enable chatbots to interpret and process user language, including identifying user intent, detecting emotional cues, and understanding semantic context. The system provides the necessary components for creating human-like dialogue systems which detect psychological indicators such as distress and anxiety and rumination (Zhang & Luo, 2024).
Therapeutic Content Library: This curated repository contains psychoeducational materials, CBT-based exercises, mood-tracking prompts, mindfulness activities, journaling modules, and coping strategies. The chatbot uses the library to create interventions which match the user’s present emotional condition and their therapeutic objectives.
Personalization and Memory System: The advanced mental health chatbots use a memory system which allows them to save and retrieve information about users including their past interactions and their mood patterns and their identified stress factors. The system enables customized user experiences which lead to ongoing user engagement. The chatbot uses its previous information about anxiety triggers to check on staff member progress towards their complete treatment objectives through digital therapeutic activities which help to build trust between them and their clients (Zhang & Luo, 2024).
Emotion Detection and Adaptive Responses: The chatbot uses AI-based sentiment analysis and emotion recognition technology to change its vocal delivery and information presentation according to the emotional state of the user. The system responds to sadness by using three techniques which include decreasing its speaking speed and using more compassionate terminology and offering grounding methods. The system creates interactions which feel more authentic through its ability to respond with human empathy and understanding (MindLift study, 2025).
Data Storage and Security Layer: The protection of mental health information needs strong security measures because this data is highly sensitive. Most platforms use encrypted databases to store user data securely, complying with data protection standards such as GDPR or HIPAA. The basis of mental health treatment methods relies on trust because patients require assurance of their information protection to share their mental health status with professionals.
