2.8.	 Related Works
The authors of the study Mishra and Ajwani (2022) provided a theoretical framework for their analysis of AI therapist systems. The authors argued that the systems would provide benefits through their ability to protect user identities while delivering healthcare services at all times and increasing access to mental health support for multiple individuals. The research paper demonstrated that the systems would decrease treatment access barriers which would result in reduced psychological distress for patients. The researchers failed to validate their theories through practical testing or clinical evaluations. The study lacks knowledge about system effectiveness because the researchers conducted their assessment of systems through laboratory conditions instead of actual therapeutic environments which should involve patient engagement for mental health assessment according to study requirements.
Farzan et al. (2021) conducted a systematic review of ten empirical studies on chatbot-delivered CBT interventions, covering well-known platforms such as Woebot, Wysa, and Youper. The review found moderate improvements in symptoms of anxiety and depression, demonstrating the potential of chatbot-assisted therapy. The studies that were examined showed considerable differences between their evaluation methods and their testing periods and their chatbot system capabilities. This lack of standardized evaluation frameworks highlights the need for more uniform approaches that can reliably assess how chatbot design influences therapeutic engagement and clinical efficacy an approach adopted in this research.
The research of Joshi et al. (2023) studied the creation and execution of AI-powered mental health chatbots while they investigated technical difficulties and ethical issues that included user withdrawal and restricted emotional reaction and shortcomings in customized responses. The discussion provided useful practical knowledge to the audience but lacked empirical evidence because the researchers did not conduct any user or clinical environment tests. The current research fills an existing gap which shows how different design elements of chatbots affect user behavior and emotional ties and treatment results through scientific testing.
Farzan et al. (2025) conducted a small study which randomly assigned teenagers to use either a CBT chatbot or a control group. The group using the chatbot showed fewer symptoms of depression than the control group which indicates that the chatbot may provide useful assistance. The results from this study can only be used to generalize findings about other populations because it used a small sample size of 18 participants and had a short study duration. Researchers need to conduct larger studies with participants from various backgrounds who will use the chatbot to determine whether the positive findings continue across different age groups and backgrounds.
The researchers Baek et al. (2025) conducted their investigation of previous research about AI chatbots which provide emotional support to healthcare professionals. The research demonstrated that healthcare workers experienced reduced stress levels and improved mood through their usage of chatbots. The research studies which they examined did not show a strong resemblance to each other. The studies exhibited multiple distinctions across their participants study designs and their evaluation techniques. The findings remained uncertain because of this situation. The existence of inconsistent findings has created a requirement for establishing a standardized measurement approach which all parties must adopt. This approach will enable us to deliver consistent positive outcomes throughout all occurrences. The research follows this concept which requires established procedures as its main research direction.
Casu et al. (2024) conducted a scoping review which evaluated fifteen studies about artificial intelligence chatbots in mental healthcare. The findings showed positive outcomes for emotional well-being because the system effectively managed worry and sadness. The research faced two major obstacles because of the limited availability of randomized controlled trials and the researchers' failure to investigate long-term effects. The studies investigated how users affected chatbot operations and their ability to deliver customized emotional responses which were two rare research areas. The study will investigate how customizable chatbot dialogues affect user happiness and their sustained mental well-being.
The Fido Study (2023) conducted a randomized controlled study with 81 persons to test a bilingual, CBT chatbot on phones and desktops. The study results showed that the chatbot reduced symptoms of depression and anxiety. The users achieved better results when they used the chatbot more frequently. The study only detected participant improvement at two and four week intervals which prevents us from assessing the long-term value of the chatbot. The study failed to examine how the chatbot design elements affected user retention. Our research requires these elements because we will develop an advanced chatbot through better dialogue capabilities and design improvements before we evaluate its performance in actual usage. 
Jiang et al. (2024) reviewed how large language models (LLMs) can be employed in CBT chatbots. They claimed that LLMs could simulate therapeutic conversations while they showed emotional responses and created individualized user solutions. Their research existed only as a theoretical framework that lacked experimental evidence. The paper remained unverified via peer review. LLB-based CBT chatbots need authentic demonstrations that showcase their actual functioning according to this requirement. Our work seeks to meet this demand through trials.
Conclusion
The current studies demonstrate various difficulties in the development and operation of mental health chatbots. Most chatbots deliver identical responses which contain basic information about their services according to their predefined scripts which their users already know. People find it difficult to trust the chatbot because they lose interest while using it which results in decreased ability to receive assistance from the system. The research problem currently exists because various studies use different methods to assess credibility yet the main research problem requires chatbots to provide responses which show deep understanding and empathy toward users. This research creates a CBT chatbot which provides users with tailored emotional support through its automated emotional intelligence functions to solve this research problem. The research adopts user-centered design because it tests how better personalization helps users stay engaged while enhancing their mental health results.

