3.1.	Research Design
The research designed applied was development and evaluative research methods to conduct their study. The process proceeded through multiple cycles which included design work followed by implementation testing and system improvement work. The approach needed to be executed because it established the technical foundation which enabled the system to operate effectively as a therapeutic tool before proceeding to full evaluation. 
The project selected specific technologies which enabled users to interact with the system in real time while performing advanced natural language processing tasks and providing structured yet adaptable communication needed for Cognitive Behavioral Therapy practices. The system was developed as a web application which includes distinct frontend and backend components.
 The user interface of the frontend system was built using JavaScript together with the React.js framework. This combination was selected because it enabled the development of interactive single-page applications which maintain user interest throughout therapy sessions.
 The backend system includes server-side processing and artificial intelligence functions which was built with JavaScript and Node.js. JavaScript was selected because it provides an extensive range of reliable machine learning and natural language processing libraries which support user input processing and sentiment analysis and empathetic response generation based on cognitive behavioral therapy principles.  
3.2. 	Research Population and Sampling
3.2.1. 	Study Population
The study population consists of adults aged 18 and above who self-report experiencing mild to moderate emotional distress, such as symptoms of anxiety, stress, or depression. This group represents the intended end-users of the AI-powered CBT chatbot, as they are most likely to benefit from accessible, early-intervention mental health support.
3.2.2. 	Sampling Technique
A purposive sample strategy was applied to recruit participants. The non-probability sampling method used in this research allows researchers to select specific participants who fulfill particular requirements that match their study objectives. The researchers will recruit participants who have experienced mild to moderate emotional distress and who are willing to use digital mental health services. The sample selection method ensures that participants meet the chatbot's operational requirements which also enhances the accuracy of evaluation feedback collected during the assessment process.
3.2.3. 	Sample Size
A sample size of 20 to 30 participants was targeted for this study. This range was judged ideal for an exploratory pilot study of this sort, since it allows for the collection of rich, significant qualitative and quantitative data while being logistically viable.   The sample size is consistent with those utilized in similar developmental and usability studies within the field of digital mental health therapies. 
3.3. 	Data Collection Methods
A mixed-methods data collection technique was adopted for the creation and assessment of the AI-powered CBT chatbot. The strategy blended clinical expert qualitative insights with user quantitative and qualitative feedback, which produced a dependable framework for both product development and testing. 
The semi-structured interview with a professional therapist functioned as the major source of information which led the construction of the chatbot. The interview strategy devised served to obtain expert information about key CBT principles and their implementation in digital media. 
After the development phase, user data collecting happened to test the system's operating capabilities. The evaluation used a post-interaction self-administered questionnaire as the major assessment method. The questionnaire was provided digitally after participants finished a set period of interaction with the chatbot. The paper was meant to collect demographic data and evaluate usability and establish perceived advantages. The full set of questionnaire items is presented in Appendix I, while the analytical results and user feedback received from the survey are provided in Appendix II.
3.4. 	Data Analysis
A mixed-methods approach was applied to analyze the obtained data, providing for a thorough grasp of both statistical trends and detailed user experiences.   This blend of quantitative and qualitative methodologies provides robust insights into the usability, effectiveness, and therapeutic relevance of the chatbot.  
Quantitative data obtained from the Google Forms survey items (see Appendix I for the questionnaire and Appendix II for the collected results) were analyzed using descriptive statistics, including measures of central tendency and variability such as means, standard deviations, and frequency distributions.   These analyses summarized participants' evaluations of usability, usefulness, and therapeutic relationship.   
Qualitative data from open-ended survey responses (Appendix II), was examined using theme analysis.   This method comprised familiarization with the data, generating initial codes, searching for themes, assessing prospective themes, defining and identifying them, and producing a structured report of the findings.   This technique revealed repeating trends in user feedback, including perceived benefits of the chatbot, emotional responsiveness, and areas for improvement.  
By blending quantitative trends with qualitative depth, this mixed-methods research presented a multi-faceted review of the chatbot’s performance, emphasizing not only how users connected with the system but also why particular design features succeeded or required refining  
